\p{[Retained laws](fdip-0018) combine Schur elimination and Gaussian
integration; [backgrounds](fdip-0019) then specify which data and laws
are transported. [Half-turn sectors](fdip-001A) lead to
[finite-group symmetry](fdip-004R), with complete real projectors
and cyclic and dihedral examples. [Equivariant precision](fdip-004Y)
then resolves multiplicities over the reals, complexes and quaternions,
with a twisted prism and a binary tetrahedral Cayley graph.
The [Gaussian sector laws](fdip-0054) give independent factors and
equivariant elimination; cycle decimation computes a stable retained
set whose precision is induced rather than restricted.
The finite-group action preserves the
unpartitioned graph precision; a retained subset would additionally
have to be stable. [Clifford algebras](fdip-001B)
use the earlier constructions; the sector comparison is restated locally in
the Clifford chapter. [Spatial Dirac](fdip-001C),
[enriched comparison](fdip-001E) and [arithmetic](fdip-001D) develop
different subsequent questions, with relative precision restated in the
arithmetic setting. The [circle approximation](fdip-004G) uses the
foundation pairings and Gaussian covariance, with spatial Dirac as
optional comparison. [Realization and observables](fdip-004L) use
incidence, retained laws, enriched transport and the arithmetic
chapter's fiber criterion; the triangle illustrates the realization
choice. [Selected backgrounds and invariants](fdip-0059) then allow arbitrary
orthogonal retained subspaces, derive their Gaussian laws and
distinguish graph arrows from wider orthogonal comparisons. The
four-cycle and quaternion examples compare coordinate and group-algebra
selectors. Their retained spectra distinguish the resulting laws;
the restriction criterion and prism example explain which symmetry
selectors preserve a precision by restriction. Isotropy pieces record
graph stabilizers without asserting physical phase structure.
The [induced action](fdip-005Q) integrates the full Gaussian field;
its edge derivatives, concavity and rooted-forest expansion connect
the normalizer to graph observables. A finite-cycle determinant
keeps the circle scaling and reference-measure convention explicit.
Conditional Gaussian elimination then produces
[background-dependent interactions](fdip-005Y). Their formal
coefficients transport with the interaction; a quartic cycle exhibits
the induced vertices and their dependence on the selector.
The [local Gaussian laws](fdip-0063) use marginalization and conditional
precision to study gluing. Separator products give a unique Markov law
on a chordal clique cover; a four-cycle shows why overlap consistency
alone does not suffice. This is a finite gluing statement for laws,
with a separate boundary from descent of background objects.
The [connection chapter](fdip-006C) replaces ordinary differences by
unitary transport along edges. Gauge covariance and cycle holonomies
lead to exact determinants and a normalized complex circle action;
antiperiodic spatial modes separate exact zero modes from additional
low-energy branches.
The [finite gauge-field chapter](fdip-006L) then supplies square faces
and sums over edge connections with a Wilson action. Finite averaging
and the exact two-element torus law distinguish gauge invariance,
global holonomies and the pure-gauge area-law limit.
The [surface character formula](fdip-006U) extends the exact
partition calculation to noncommutative finite groups on closed
oriented surfaces. Edge orthogonality and vertex index sums fix
the normalization; quaternion and binary tetrahedral examples
give distinct spinor Wilson laws.
For [matter in a fixed connection](fdip-006Z), an absolutely
convergent closed-walk expansion computes the determinant action.
Its leading scalar plaquette coefficient, short-period winding terms
and link-current derivative retain the real or complex normalization.
The [joint matter and connection law](fdip-0075) then makes the
connection dynamical. Its exact marginal and conditional measures,
a small-torus determinant sum and a positive-loop comparison separate
finite matter effects from claims about asymptotic screening.
The [compact-group chapter](fdip-007A) replaces edge sums by Haar
integrals and states the convergence needed for surface character
expansions. Circle weights distinguish matching one Fourier coefficient
from matching a law; the classical smooth-field calculation fixes
Wilson-action normalization. Quaternion double covers and explicit
representation data separate internal fields from substrate actions.
The [reflection-positivity chapter](fdip-007L) chooses a time
direction and distinguishes site and link reflections. A scalar
Gaussian criterion, its cutoff proof and a two-site Gram matrix
explain positive reflected pairings. A six-site product law shows
why those pairings alone do not give a quotient time shift.
The [Gaussian transfer operator](fdip-007S) then supplies an
explicit time step. Hermite diagonalization fixes its leading
eigenvalue; periodic traces reproduce the cycle determinant and
covariance. Normalization produces a spectral logarithm with the
domain and gap of a harmonic oscillator.
The [synthesis](fdip-003W) compares the earlier finite constructions
with the conjecture. The opening motivation and forward example links
orient these readings without adding hypotheses.}
